.TH LIBSTRARRAY_STRARRAY_AT 3 "2023-04-01" "libstrarray" "String Array Library"
.SH NAME
strarray_at \- Get the element at a specified index in a string array
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <libstrarray.h>
.sp
.B char*
.B strarray_at(strarray* stra, size_t n);
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B strarray_at
function returns the element at the specified index in a string array. The
.B stra
argument is a pointer to a
.B strarray
structure representing the array, and the
.B n
argument is a size_t representing the index of the element to retrieve.
The
.B strarray_at
function returns a pointer to the element at the specified index, or NULL if the index is out of bounds.
.SH EXAMPLE
The following example demonstrates how to use the
.B strarray_at
function to retrieve an element from a string array:
.nf
.B #include <libstrarray.h>
.B #include <stdio.h>
.sp
.B int main() {
.B   strarray arr;
.B   strarray_init(&arr);
.B   strarray_append(&arr, "Hello");
.B   strarray_append(&arr, "world");
.B   char* element = strarray_at(&arr, 1);
.B   printf("Element at index 1: %s\n", element);
.B   // Free the allocated memory
.B   strarray_done(&arr);
.B   return 0;
.B }
.ne
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.B strarray_init(3),
.B strarray_append(3),
.B strarray_done(3)